This is the classic `torch.compile` prefix problem. `_orig_mod.` gets added to every key when you save from a compiled model, or when you load into a compiled model without stripping it if the checkpoint was from an uncompiled one. The cleanest fix is to preprocess the state_dict keys before loading. Define a simple mapping function:

```python
def strip_orig_mod_prefix(state_dict):
    new_state_dict = {}
    for k, v in state_dict.items():
        if k.startswith('_orig_mod.'):
            new_state_dict[k[len('_orig_mod.'):]] = v
        else:
            new_state_dict[k] = v
    return new_state_dict

# Usage:
checkpoint = torch.load('your_checkpoint.pt')
model.load_state_dict(strip_orig_mod_prefix(checkpoint['model_state_dict']))
```

This makes your loading logic robust to whether the checkpoint came from a compiled or uncompiled model, or if you're loading into one. Don't manually rename things in your checkpoint file; fix the loading logic.

